Item 6: OS matrix. Adding Windows and macOS legs catches platform-specific bugs — path handling, line endings, fork() vs spawn, native deps, subprocess behavior — that a Linux-only matrix will never surface.

1. The naive change (and why it's expensive)

You could just extend the existing matrix:

```yaml
matrix:
  os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest, macos-latest]
  python-version: [ "3.10", "3.11", "3.12" ]
```

That's 9 jobs, each ~2–5 min → ~30 min of runner time per push, and macOS runners bill at 10× the Linux rate on private repos. For most projects that's overkill.

2. Recommended approach: hybrid matrix

Run the full Python matrix on Linux, and a single representative Python version on Windows and macOS:

```yaml
  test:
    name: Test (${{ matrix.os }}, Python ${{ matrix.python-version }})
    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}

    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        include:
          # Full Python matrix on Linux
          - os: ubuntu-latest
            python-version: "3.10"
          - os: ubuntu-latest
            python-version: "3.11"
          - os: ubuntu-latest
            python-version: "3.12"
          # One representative version on Windows and macOS
          - os: windows-latest
            python-version: "3.12"
          - os: macos-latest
            python-version: "3.12"

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}
          cache: 'pip'

      - name: Install dependencies
        shell: bash
        run: |
          python -m pip install --upgrade pip
          pip install pytest pytest-cov
          if [ -f requirements.txt ]; then pip install -r requirements.txt; fi

      - name: Test with pytest
        shell: bash
        run: pytest --cov=. --cov-report=xml --cov-report=term-missing

      - name: Upload coverage to Codecov
        if: matrix.os == 'ubuntu-latest' && matrix.python-version == '3.12'
        uses: codecov/codecov-action@v4
        with:
          files: ./coverage.xml
          fail_ci_if_error: false
          token: ${{ secrets.CODECOV_TOKEN }}
```

That gives you 5 jobs instead of 9, still covering all three OSes, and only one coverage upload.

Key details

· shell: bash on all steps. Windows defaults to PowerShell, which breaks if [ -f requirements.txt ]. Forcing bash (Git Bash ships with every Windows runner) makes the same commands work everywhere.
· if: matrix.os == 'ubuntu-latest' && ... — only upload coverage once. Otherwise three Codecov uploads race and you get confusing merge behavior.
· fail-fast: false — keep it, so a Windows-only failure doesn't cancel the other legs while you're debugging.
· setup-python cache works on all three OSes with cache: 'pip'; it keys off your requirements*.txt / pyproject.toml hashes.

3. Update branch protection

Required checks now include the new names:

· Lint
· Test (ubuntu-latest, Python 3.10)
· Test (ubuntu-latest, Python 3.11)
· Test (ubuntu-latest, Python 3.12)
· Test (windows-latest, Python 3.12)
· Test (macos-latest, Python 3.12)
· Security

The names must match exactly. Since you're using ${{ matrix.os }} and ${{ matrix.python-version }} in the job name, the strings will be ubuntu-latest, windows-latest, macos-latest — not Linux, Windows, etc. Update the ruleset JSON (item 5) to match, or merges will silently stop being blocked by the OS legs.

4. Common platform pitfalls this catches

· Path separators — hardcoded / in file paths fails on Windows.
· Case sensitivity — macOS and Windows are case-insensitive by default; import Foo vs foo.py passes there but fails on Linux.
· Line endings — git core.autocrlf=true on Windows changes \n to \r\n. Add a .gitattributes if you read/write fixtures:
  ```
  * text=auto eol=lf
  *.png binary
  ```
· fork() vs spawn — multiprocessing defaults differ; passing unpicklable objects works on Linux, fails on Windows/macOS.
· Signal handling — SIGKILL, SIGHUP don't exist on Windows; signal.SIGTERM behaves differently.
· Native extension builds — wheels that exist for Linux may not exist for macOS arm64 (macos-latest is Apple Silicon since late 2023). Missing wheels → source build → often fails.
· File locking — Windows locks open files, so os.remove() on a file another handle holds raises PermissionError.

If your project is pure-Python and has no OS-specific code, the Windows/macOS legs will be green forever and just burn minutes. In that case, use option 2's include: list but skip the extra legs entirely — don't add an OS matrix you don't need.

5. macOS architecture note

macos-latest is Apple Silicon (arm64) since GitHub switched the default in 2023. If you support Intel Macs, add an explicit leg:

```yaml
          - os: macos-13        # Intel
            python-version: "3.12"
          - os: macos-latest    # Apple Silicon
            python-version: "3.12"
```

macos-13 is the last Intel image GitHub publishes; keep an eye on deprecation notices.

6. Save minutes: skip docs-only changes

OS matrices are the expensive part of CI. Add a path filter to the whole workflow or a specific job so README tweaks don't spin up 5 runners:

```yaml
on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main", "master" ]
    paths-ignore:
      - 'docs/**'
      - '**.md'
      - 'LICENSE'
  pull_request:
    branches: [ "main", "master" ]
    paths-ignore:
      - 'docs/**'
      - '**.md'
      - 'LICENSE'
```

Caveat from item 5: if test is a required check, paths-ignore will make the check never report on docs-only PRs — which blocks the merge forever. Two options:

1. Don't use paths-ignore on required workflows.
2. Add an "always-pass" stub job that runs when the real job is skipped:

```yaml
  test-stub:
    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request' && contains(github.event.pull_request.changed_files, '') == false
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: echo "no code changes"
```

Option 1 is simpler. Most teams just accept a couple of wasted runner minutes on README PRs.

7. Full workflow, assembled

```yaml
