8. Cost sanity check

Rough runner-minute cost per push (public repo → free; private repo → billed):

Config Jobs Avg min/job Total min Notes
Linux-only 3.10/3.11/3.12 3 3 9 baseline
+ Windows 3.12 4 5 15 Windows ~1.5× slower
+ macOS 3.12 5 4 25 macOS billed at 10×
Full 3×3 9 4 36 usually not worth it

If your project has no OS-specific code, the two extra legs are pure overhead. Add them when you have a reason — a bug report from Windows, a native dep, path-sensitive code — not by default.

9. Gotchas recap

· shell: bash on Windows is non-negotiable for if [ -f ... ] style commands.
· Required check names change when you change the job name — update branch protection / ruleset JSON in the same PR.
· paths-ignore + required checks = deadlock. Pick one.
· macos-latest is arm64. If you need Intel, pin macos-13 and watch for its EOL.
· Cache keys are per-OS automatically with setup-python@v5 — you don't need to do anything, but be aware caches don't share across OSes.
· Case-insensitive filesystems on macOS/Windows hide import bugs that Linux catches. Don't rely on the extra legs to find them; keep the Linux leg.
· Line endings creep in via git on Windows. A .gitattributes with * text=auto eol=lf prevents most of it.

