8. Gotchas

· Silent no-op caches. cache: 'pip' with no matching dependency file caches nothing and doesn't warn. Always set cache-dependency-path if your deps don't live in requirements.txt / pyproject.toml.
· Cross-OS cache pollution. GitHub isolates caches per OS at the storage layer, but if you build custom actions/cache keys, forgetting ${{ runner.os }} means a Linux wheel can land on Windows.
· actions/cache@v4 is the current major. v2/v3 are deprecated and will be shut off; if you have old workflows with v2, upgrade now.
· Cache-miss ≠ cache-key-miss. If the restore succeeds but the restore-key prefix doesn't match the exact key, you get a partial restore — usually what you want, but occasionally the source of "works locally, fails in CI" mysteries. Test by temporarily deleting the cache via gh cache delete.
· Monorepo bloat. A 10 GB cap per repo, LRU eviction. Big pip caches can evict your pre-commit cache; if you see flapping, split caches across jobs or scope more narrowly.
· Don't cache dist/ or build/. They're build outputs, not dependencies — caching them causes stale-wheel bugs.

