Next → item 8: scheduled runs (nightly cron) to catch dependency rot, plus how to make the security job run daily without spamming PRs. Or tell me to jump elsewhere.Item 8: scheduled runs. Nightly (or weekly) CI catches things that push/PR triggers can't — dependency rot, upstream CVE disclosures, breaking changes in transitive deps, and OS image updates that silently change your environment.

1. Why schedule a run at all

Push/PR triggers only fire when you change something. That means:

· A CVE drops in a dependency you've been happily using for six months — you won't know until the next PR happens to run.
· A transitive dep gets yanked from PyPI — your cached wheel keeps working until it doesn't.
· GitHub rotates the ubuntu-latest image and Python 3.10's openssl version changes — you find out on your next release PR.
· A Dependabot PR sits unmerged for weeks and you don't notice the world moved.

A nightly run gives you a known-good baseline independent of your commit cadence.

2. Cron syntax on GitHub Actions

```yaml
on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 3 * * *"     # 03:00 UTC daily
```

Rules that trip people up:

· Always UTC. No timezone field. 0 3 * * * is 3am UTC — 7pm PT previous day, 4am CET, 11am JST. Pick a slot that overlaps someone's workday, or at least a slot where a human will see a red build before Monday.
· Minimum interval is 5 minutes (*/5 * * * *). Anything finer is silently rounded.
· Schedules are best-effort. GitHub delays runs during peak load; a "3am" job often starts at 3:15–3:40. Don't build anything that depends on the exact start time.
· Scheduled workflows only run on the default branch. If your default is main, a schedule on a feature branch won't fire. This also means git push to master-named default still works but the cron entry must be on the default branch's copy of the file.
· 60-day inactivity auto-disable. If nobody pushes to the repo for 60 days, GitHub pauses all scheduled workflows and emails the repo admins. You must re-enable manually (or push a commit). For low-traffic repos, add a "keepalive" job that pushes an empty commit monthly, or use the gautamkrishnar/keepalive-workflow action.

3. Don't just add schedule: to the CI workflow

If you add schedule: to python-app.yml, the full OS matrix, lint, and security jobs all run nightly. That's usually overkill — the OS legs exist to catch platform bugs on new code, not to re-verify a static repo every night.

Two better patterns:

Pattern A: separate nightly.yml — focused, cheap, one job.

Pattern B: gate jobs on the trigger.

```yaml
  test-full:
    if: github.event_name != 'schedule'
  test-quick:
    if: github.event_name == 'schedule'
```

Pattern A is cleaner. Most projects do this.

4. Recommended nightly workflow

.github/workflows/nightly.yml

```yaml
name: Nightly

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 3 * * *"     # 03:00 UTC daily
  workflow_dispatch:        # allow manual trigger from the Actions tab

concurrency:
  group: ${{ github.workflow }}
  cancel-in-progress: false   # don't kill a nightly mid-flight

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  dependency-audit:
    name: Dependency audit
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"
          cache: 'pip'
          cache-dependency-path: requirements*.txt

      - name: Install
        run: |
          python -m pip install --upgrade pip
          pip install pip-audit
          if [ -f requirements.txt ]; then pip install -r requirements.txt; fi

      - name: pip-audit — CVEs against today's advisory DB
        run: pip-audit --strict --desc on

  freshness-test:
    name: Install with latest allowed deps
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"
          # Note: no pip cache — we deliberately want fresh downloads.

      - name: Install with --upgrade
        run: |
          python -m pip install --upgrade pip
          pip install pytest
          if [ -f requirements.txt ]; then
            # Ignore pins, get whatever is newest that satisfies specifiers.
            pip install --upgrade -r requirements.txt
          fi

      - name: Test
        run: pytest -q

  # Optional: notify on failure so it doesn't silently go red for a week.
  notify:
    name: Notify on failure
    needs: [dependency-audit, freshness-test]
    if: failure()
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Open or update tracking issue
        uses: actions/github-script@v7
        with:
          script: |
            const title = "Nightly build failing";
            const body = `Run: ${context.serverUrl}/${context.repo.owner}/${context.repo.repo}/actions/runs/${context.runId}`;
            const { data: issues } = await github.rest.issues.listForRepo({
              owner: context.repo.owner,
              repo: context.repo.repo,
              state: "open",
              labels: "nightly-failure",
            });
            if (issues.length) {
              await github.rest.issues.createComment({
                owner: context.repo.owner,
                repo: context.repo.repo,
                issue_number: issues[0].number,
                body,
              });
            } else {
              await github.rest.issues.create({
                owner: context.repo.owner,
                repo: context.repo.repo,
                title,
                body,
                labels: ["nightly-failure"],
              });
            }
```

What each job is doing

dependency-audit — same as your security job but without the Bandit source scan. Source code doesn't change overnight; CVEs do. Runs pip-audit --strict, which will fail the moment a new advisory hits.

freshness-test — installs deps without honoring the lock/pin, then runs the test suite. This is what catches "your >=1.2,<2 constraint is now satisfied by a broken 1.9.3". Skipping the pip cache is deliberate — you want fresh PyPI downloads.

notify — a failure that nobody sees is worse than no CI. This opens (or comments on) a tracked issue so it shows up in your repo's Issues tab, not just buried in Actions history.

If you don't want issue churn, swap the notify job for a Slack webhook:

```yaml
      - name: Slack alert
        uses: slackapi/slack-github-action@v2
        with:
          webhook: ${{ secrets.SLACK_WEBHOOK }}
          webhook-type: incoming-webhook
          payload: |
            {"text": "Nightly failed: ${{ github.server_url }}/${{ github.repository }}/actions/runs/${{ github.run_id }}"}
```

5. Weekly instead of nightly

Nightly is great for the security job, less so for the freshness test — you rarely want to know about a dep regression on a Saturday. A common split:

```yaml
on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 3 * * *"     # daily: security only
    - cron: "0 6 * * 1"     # Monday 06:00 UTC: full freshness + audit
```

Then gate:

```yaml
jobs:
  security:
    if: github.event.schedule == '0 3 * * *' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
  freshness:
    if: github.event.schedule == '0 6 * * 1' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
```

github.event.schedule contains the cron string that fired, so you can branch on it. workflow_dispatch is included so a manual run executes both.

6. Making scheduled runs and branch protection coexist

If your default branch requires status checks like Test (ubuntu-latest, Python 3.12), a nightly run that fires on the default branch will report those checks — but they'll overwrite the last PR's results in the checks UI. Usually harmless. Where it does bite:

· Stale checks block merges. A PR opened right before the nightly run can pick up "the nightly's" check state and get stuck if the nightly fails for an unrelated reason. Mitigation: keep scheduled workflows out of the required-checks namespace — name the nightly jobs differently (Nightly: audit, Nightly: freshness) so they don't collide with Test (...).
· Force-pushing the default branch (e.g. from the keepalive bot) resets all check history. Don't do that on protected branches.

The clean rule: scheduled workflows should never produce a check name that's in your required-checks list.

7. Keepalive for quiet repos

If your repo has weeks of no pushes and you want the nightly cron to keep firing past 60 days:

```yaml
name: Keepalive

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 0 1 * *"     # 1st of each month

permissions:
  contents: write

jobs:
  keepalive:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.repository.default_branch }}

      - name: Empty commit to reset the 60-day timer
        run: |
          git config user.name "github-actions[bot]"
          git config user.email "github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com"
          git commit --allow-empty -m "chore: keepalive"
          git push
```

Caveat: if the default branch is protected against direct pushes, this will fail. Either add the GitHub Actions bot to the ruleset bypass list, or use the gautamkrishnar/keepalive-workflow action which handles this via workflow dispatch.

Alternatively, just accept the disable and re-enable from Actions → Nightly → Enable workflow when you notice. For active repos it's a non-issue.

8. Gotchas recap

· Cron is UTC and best-effort. Don't depend on exact timing; don't schedule at :00 if it matters (load spikes around the hour — use :17 or :41).
· Schedules only run from the default branch. Feature-branch copies are ignored.
· 60-day inactivity disables schedules. Add a keepalive if your repo is quiet.
· on: schedule alone gives you no manual trigger. Always pair with workflow_dispatch.
· Don't mix nightly into the required-checks namespace. Name nightly jobs distinctly.
· concurrency.cancel-in-progress: false for scheduled runs — a nightly run shouldn't be killed by the next one starting, especially if it's the security scan.
· A red nightly that nobody sees is a green nightly. Add the notify job or a webhook.
